\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A nonconstant plaquette weight}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Count the positive faces on the #{4\times4} grid of
\ref{fdip-019G}. At the trivial connection all #{16}
holonomies are one. Marking the single spatial link by
#{\ui} changes exactly the two adjacent faces. On one
boundary traversal the link acts forward and on the other
it acts backward, giving #{\ui} and #{-\ui}. The remaining
#{14} holonomies are one. The two real-trace sums are
therefore #{16} and #{14}.}

\p{By \ref{fdip-019H} the scalar gauge weight #{1+s_{\square}(U)^2}
is invariant under inversion on the class of these
fourth-root connections. It has different values #{257}
and #{197} at the two displayed connections. Multiplication
by it in \ref{fdip-019F} gives a determinant weight with
a nonconstant gauge factor.}
